WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Have you ever wondered
if there’s a natural way

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.160
to lower your high blood pressure,
guard against Alzheimer's,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.440
lose weight, and feel better?
Well, it turns out there is!

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.680
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.000
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.560
Today, we look at the anti-aging properties
of the diabetes drug, Metformin.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.080
The noxious weed goat's rue

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.960
was widely used
as an herbal medicine

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.960
back in the Middle Ages for
thirst and frequent urination,

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.920
what we now know of
as a cardinal symptom

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.200
and sign of diabetes.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.160
The plant produces
a mitochondrial poison

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.160
known as galegine.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.800
One can imagine
how this could help discourage

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.200
grazing herbivores,

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.400
but why would crippling
mitochondrial function

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.880
help with diabetes?

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.760
Well, if you got the dose
just right,

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.280
you could impair
energy production just enough

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.040
to spring AMPK into action.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.720
AMPK would then dial down

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.720
the liver's
energy-intensive process

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.400
of sugar production,
and blood sugars would fall.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.760
Galegine proved too toxic
for use in humans

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.520
(and presumably goats)

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.400
but was tweaked
into a milder form in the 1950s.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.600
That's the origin story
of metformin,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.600
now, even 60+ years later

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.880
the most widely prescribed
diabetes drug in the world.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.600
Sold originally as Glucophage
(meaning "sugar eater"),

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.520
metformin is
now prescribed annually

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.000
more than 90 million times
in the U.S. alone.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.520
Despite all the strides
in biotechnology,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.520
Big Pharma has yet
to come up with a safer,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.160
more effective
first-line treatment

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.960
for type 2 diabetes
than a drug

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.640
that retails
at pennies per pill.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.280
Ramping up AMPK
isn't all that metformin does

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.200
(for example, it also appears
to foster the growth

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.040
of good bacteria
in the gut).

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.880
But the AMPK boost alone
would be expected

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.240
to have
wide-ranging health effects.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.040
Remember, AMPK is activated
when resources are scarce;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.120
so, it causes the body
to hunker down

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.680
into energy-conservation mode

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.680
and put the brakes
on new construction projects.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.160
So, no more making excess
blood sugar or cholesterol.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.800
Time to start tapping
into the larder.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.600
And, indeed, metformin doesn't
just lower sugars in the blood

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.480
but cholesterol and body fat.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.720
And shutting down
the excess synthesis of proteins

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.800
can also have health benefits.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.720
Fibrosis is the accumulation
of scar tissue

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.040
in response
to an inflammatory insult.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.920
As a transient,
localized phenomenon,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.160
fibrosis helps heal wounds
and repair damaged tissue,

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.760
but in response to the systemic
inflammation of aging,

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.360
surplus scar tissue
can build up in our heart,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.760
liver, kidneys, and lungs.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.480
Excess fibrosis is estimated
to contribute to 45 percent

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.680
of all death
in the United States.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.240
But AMPK switches the body
into miser mode,

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.680
shrinking the energy budget

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.440
for such superfluous activities

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.960
and ratchets down
fibrotic processes.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.800
The AMPK priorities shift
from growth to preservation

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.080
might also be expected
to slow cancer progression.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.520
Based on dozens
of observational studies

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.520
involving tens of thousands
of diabetic cancer patients,

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.200
those on metformin
did indeed experience

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.160
a significant
survival advantage,

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:18.200
correlating with
a 26 percent decreased risk

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.200
of dying from cancer.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.200
No wonder there are
more than 100 ongoing studies

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.560
to put metformin
to the test against cancer.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.280
As the director of Harvard's
Cancer Center put it,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.280
"Metformin may have
already saved more people

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.000
from cancer deaths
than any drug in history."

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.400
If AMPK plays such a key role

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.080
in the aging process,

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.400
and metformin boosts AMPK,

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.000
can metformin slow aging

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.160
and lengthen lifespans?

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.040
It certainly can
in some species.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.360
C. elegans worms fed metformin

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.840
maintained a youthful state

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.440
and can exhibit
a nearly 40 percent increase

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.000
in average survival.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.360
Interfere with
the AMPK pathway, though,

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.920
and this
lifespan extension disappears,

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.800
confirming the role of AMPK

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.080
in metformin's
anti-aging effects.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.360
Metformin also improves

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.040
the healthspans
and lifespans of mice,

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.480
but failed to extend
the lives of rats,

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.080
calling the pro-longevity
effects of metformin

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:15.760
into question.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.240
The dose they used

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.240
(15 times that used in humans)

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.120
may have been too high, however,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.880
and the F344 rats they used

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.520
are an inbred strain

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.960
(named after the 344th
brother sister mating)

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.360
that are resistant
to the health benefits

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.160
of calorie restriction.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.080
Since metformin
acts through AMPK

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.360
as a calorie
restriction mimetic,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.120
it stands to reason
that these animals

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.320
may be less prone
to metformin's effects.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.480
What about humans?

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:45.760
Unlike other potential

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:48.160
anti-aging drugs
in the pipeline,

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.360
we have the benefit of data

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.200
on more than
a million study subjects

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.160
who have been tracked
in metformin trials.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.280
And, indeed,

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:55.560
compared to diabetics

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.640
who take drugs
other than metformin,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.440
those on metformin
appear protected

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.680
from age-related
susceptibilities,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:02.880
like fractures,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.800
or serious infections,

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.000
glaucoma,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.440
and cognitive impairment.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:09.400
Most importantly,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.840
diabetics who take metformin

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:12.520
live significantly longer

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.280
than those taking different
blood sugar-lowering drugs.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.120
Doesn't that prove metformin

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.720
is a pro-longevity drug?

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:20.400
No.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.880
For example, maybe metformin

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.440
just controlled
their diabetes better.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:26.920
Researchers still found
a survival benefit

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:28.480
for diabetics on metformin

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.760
compared to those
as well managed on other drugs,

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.920
but maybe the other drugs

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:34.600
were shortening lifespans,

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.680
and so, metformin
just looked good in comparison.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.280
How could you try
to tease out the effects?

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:41.800
What if you
compared the lifespans

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.880
of diabetics on metformin

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.160
to nondiabetics?

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.280
Now, that's not really
a fair comparison.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:50.440
Any benefits metformin has

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.600
could easily
be outweighed and masked

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.520
by the life-limiting
nature of diabetes--

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.240
unless, that is,

00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:59.760
the diabetics on metformin

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.120
actually lived longer.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.080
Longer than nondiabetics.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.000
And,
are you sitting down?

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.880
That's exactly
what the research shows.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:11.960
Diabetics placed on metformin

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.920
went on to live longer lives

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.840
than those who never
got diabetes in the first place.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.640
From a longevity standpoint,

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.280
it's as if they got lucky

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.120
to be diagnosed with diabetes,

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.600
because then, they had access
to this lifespan-enhancing drug.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:26.400
So, wait.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.200
If metformin is so powerful

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.640
as to more than offset
such a dreaded diagnosis,

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.200
should everyone
be taking metformin?

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:35.800
What are the downsides?

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:42.520
That's exactly what
I'm gonna be covering next.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:44.360
The apparent
reductions in mortality

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:45.960
from all causes put together

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.160
and diseases of aging
associated with metformin use

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:50.680
suggest that the drug metformin

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.800
could be extending
life and healthspans

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.080
by acting as a geroprotective,

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.080
or anti-aging, agent.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.520
What might the downsides be?

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:01.640
Gastrointestinal side-effects,

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:02.840
such as flatulence,

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:04.420
indigestion,
and abdominal discomfort,

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.120
are relatively common.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.880
More than half
starting metformin

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.560
report diarrhea, for example,

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.080
and a quarter experience
nausea or vomiting.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.280
About 10 percent
unknowingly taking the placebo

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:16.960
report similar symptoms, though;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:18.840
so, the actual
proportion of people

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.320
suffering such symptoms
due to the drug

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.040
is probably more like 1 in 3.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.120
Most of these effects
tend to be transient, though,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.840
and can be minimized
by taking metformin with food

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.080
and starting
at a suboptimal dose,

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:33.960
increasing gradually to target

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.760
over a period of weeks.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.360
Once-a-day
extended-release versions

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:40.840
also tend to be more tolerable.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:43.280
The most dreaded
side effect, though,

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:44.840
is lactic acidosis,

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:48.200
the potentially fatal buildup
of acid in the blood.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.920
I mean,
poisoning of the mitochondria

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:52.760
is kind of the whole point.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.760
That's what activates AMPK,

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:58.480
but it's supposed to be a mild
mitochondrial poison,

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.320
but sometimes it evidently
poisons a bit too much.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:03.080
Thankfully,

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.160
metformin-induced
lactic acidosis

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.080
is extremely rare.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.520
It has a fatality rate
of 50 percent

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.160
but only occurs
at a rate of about

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:17.080
0.03 cases
per thousand patient-years.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:19.080
That means
taking metformin for a year

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:24.680
only has about a 1 in 67,000
chance of killing you.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:27.080
Lactic acidosis
is primarily caused

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:30.040
by insufficient metformin
clearance by the kidneys;

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.000
so, risk can be minimized by,

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:32.920
for example,

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:34.440
temporarily stopping the drug

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.080
while undergoing procedures

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.000
that put the kidneys at risk,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.440
such as major surgery
or intravenous contrast dyes

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.000
used for certain
imaging procedures.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:45.960
Those with
mild to moderate kidney disease

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.680
can still benefit from metformin

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.920
at an adjusted dose.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.160
Elderly individuals

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:53.680
should have their
kidney function tested

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.840
before starting metformin,
and regularly monitored.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:58.947
Excess alcohol consumption

00:08:58.947 --> 00:09:00.480
is also believed
to increase the risk

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.080
of fatal metformin-induced
lactic acidosis.

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.360
One of the more
insidious side effects

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.000
is B12 deficiency,

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:09.520
vitamin B12 deficiency,

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.080
affecting as many
as one in three

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:13.120
long-term metformin users,

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.640
but can start
as early as a few months.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:20.280
The mechanism is still unclear,

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:23.520
but routine monitoring of B12
status has been recommended,

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.120
and B12 supplementation
has been suggested.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.560
Another adverse consequence
of metformin

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:32.920
is less side effect
than main effect.

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:36.760
Remember how metformin
is a mild mitochondrial poison?

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:38.240
The way metformin boosts AMPK

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:41.920
is by impairing our body's
ability to produce energy.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.480
What might that mean
for athletic performance?

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.200
Healthy men and women
were found to suffer

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.880
a significant decline
in peak aerobic capacity

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.600
after only about
a week on metformin.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.760
The average hit was only
three percent, though,

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.480
which could be meaningful
for an elite athlete

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:01.520
but would be unlikely to impede

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:03.680
recreational physical activity.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:06.800
However, some individuals
lost as much as 10 percent,

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.760
which could
be noticeably fatiguing.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:11.520
Note these detriments
were only found

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.760
at or near maximal workloads,

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:15.360
and so, most active individuals

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:17.760
would be unlikely
to experience an impairment

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:19.400
in routine activity.

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.240
Over the long term, though,

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.400
metformin may undercut
physical fitness.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.320
Older adults were randomized

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:28.840
to 12 weeks
of supervised aerobic exercise

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.920
on or off metformin.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:33.040
They trained for 45 minutes
three times a week

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:34.920
on a treadmill,
bike, or elliptical,

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.560
ramping up to a vigorous target

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:39.400
of 85 percent
of their maximal heart rate.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:42.280
Unfortunately, metformin
attenuated the improvement

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:45.400
in physiological function
after exercise training.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:47.720
Cardiovascular fitness
is so important;

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.480
yet, after three months,
metformin cut their improvement

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:54.400
in cardiorespiratory fitness
in half compared to placebo,

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:57.680
though this didn't reach
statistical significance.

00:10:57.680 --> 00:10:59.280
Metformin did, however,

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:00.720
significantly
blunt their improvement

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:02.800
in whole-body
insulin sensitivity

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.040
which normally improves
with physical activity.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:08.080
Aerobic exercise, (or "cardio")

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:11.240
is critical for maintaining
cardiovascular fitness,

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:13.000
whereas resistance exercise

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:14.480
(also known
as strength training,

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.120
for example,
with weights or bands)

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:19.440
is essential for maintaining
muscle mass as we age.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:20.440
In 2019,

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.800
the so-called MASTERS trial
was published,

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:26.040
investigating what metformin
might do to muscle gains,

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.960
and, the title of the paper
gives it away:

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:31.440
"Metformin blunts
muscle hypertrophy

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.600
in response to progressive
resistance exercise training

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.600
in older adults."

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:38.160
After 14 weeks of progressive
resistance training,

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:39.880
men and women
in the placebo group

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:42.000
accrued significant
lean body mass,

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:45.320
but those randomized
to the metformin group did not.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:47.080
For example,
about a four percent gain

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:49.360
in thigh muscle mass
in the placebo group

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:52.080
versus virtually none
in the metformin group.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:53.760
The differences
in strength, however,

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:56.440
did not reach
statistical significance.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:58.400
So, might the benefits
of metformin

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:01.600
outweigh the risks for expanding
the healthspan and lifespan

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:04.160
of even nondiabetics?

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:05.840
The investigators
reviewing the evidence

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:08.720
that diabetics
taking metformin lived longer,

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:11.400
on average, than nondiabetics,
concluded their

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.160
"results suggest that metformin

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:14.640
could be an effective
intervention

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.280
to extend
the lifespan of people

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.640
who do not have diabetes."

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:23.520
You can't know, though,
until you put it to the test,

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.880
which I'll explore, next.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:28.680
Given the evidence

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:31.040
that diabetics
taking the drug metformin

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:32.200
may live longer

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:34.280
than people who
don't even have diabetes

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:37.160
suggests metformin
has an anti-aging effect,

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:40.080
slowing the diseases of aging
and, in doing so,

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.640
extending both
health and lifespans,

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:44.680
but you don't know

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:46.880
until you put it to the test.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:49.440
There are a few small,
brief trials of metformin

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:51.480
on healthy volunteers,
for example,

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:53.400
randomizing
a dozen or so individuals

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.080
to metformin
or placebo for a few weeks,

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:59.400
comparing the effects
on muscle and fat biopsies.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:01.240
Beneficial changes
in gene expression

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.400
in various
aging pathways were noted,

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:05.880
along with objective
improvements in risk factors,

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:08.840
such as LDL cholesterol
and triglycerides,

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:11.160
but what we'd really like
are hard endpoints,

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.160
like disease outcomes and death.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:16.240
Enter the TAME trial,

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:19.440
Targeting Aging with Metformin.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:21.000
Spearheaded by the nonprofit

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.360
American Federation
for Aging Research,

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:25.440
TAME has the ambitious goal

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:28.760
of randomizing 3,000
nondiabetic individuals

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:30.840
aged 65-79

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:32.520
to metformin or placebo

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:34.400
over a period of six years

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:36.760
to assess the effect
on age-related conditions,

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:38.040
such as heart attacks,

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:39.200
stroke,

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:40.040
cancer,

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:41.000
dementia,

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:42.680
and death.

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:45.360
Current treatments
"just exchange one disease

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.840
for another," railed
the principal investigator.

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:49.440
Someone cured of cancer

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:52.080
may just drop dead the next year
from a heart attack.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:55.720
"What we want to show
is that if we delay aging,

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:59.120
that's the best way
to delay disease."

00:13:59.120 --> 00:13:59.800
Sadly,

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.800
though the TAME trial
received FDA approval in 2015,

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:06.320
it has yet
to get off the ground.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:07.640
You can guess why.

00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:10.080
As the editor-in-chief
of an anti-aging journal put it,

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:13.760
"There is no way in hell
to make money out of it."

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:16.320
Metformin's patent
expired decades ago,

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:19.600
and generics retail
at pennies a pill.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.120
It's considered
a "genuinely good use

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:23.680
of 75 million dollars,"

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:25.320
but the researchers
have only evidently

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:27.000
been able to raise $11 million

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:29.520
from private donors.

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:31.560
Some in Silicon Valley
tech circles

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.480
aren't waiting for the results.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.460
Apparently, some techies
have started taking metformin

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:37.160
in a bid to live longer,

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:39.480
though it's only officially
available by prescription

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:41.400
for diabetes.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:43.940
Metformin is cheap,
relatively safe,

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.240
and one of the most
promising drugs to combat

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:47.880
age-related diseases,

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:49.600
but there are reasons

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:52.080
to temper our expectations.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.240
Though it's been referred
to as a "magic drug,"

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:57.080
the longevity benefits in mice

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:59.240
were small and dose dependent.

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:01.240
At best, it may increase
the average lifespan

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:03.480
of certain mice by five percent,

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:07.480
but at a higher dose,
actually shortens lifespan.

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:09.880
Much of the enthusiasm
for the TAME trial

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:12.520
rests on it acting
as a test case

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:15.320
to successfully get regulatory
authorities, like the FDA,

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:17.520
to formally recognize
any treatment

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:19.480
for the aging process itself,

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:21.800
in hopes that this will
arouse Big Pharma

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:25.200
to invest large-scale R&amp;D
to advance the field.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:28.080
Further reservations
about the panacean prospects

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:31.080
placed on metformin
arise from the landmark

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.240
Diabetes
Prevention Program study

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:36.240
published in the New England
Journal of Medicine.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.720
Thousands of prediabetic
individuals were randomized

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:41.000
to metformin
or placebo for years

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:43.520
to see if the drug
could prevent their prediabetes

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:46.320
from turning
into full-blown diabetes.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:47.520
It worked,

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:50.680
reducing overall
diabetes incidence by 31 percent

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:52.160
compared to placebo,

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:53.560
but it didn't for everyone.

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:55.960
For example, it didn't appear
to significantly benefit

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:57.800
those 60 years or older

00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:00.200
and only seemed
to help those most at risk--

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:02.040
those with class II obesity

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:04.000
and the highest blood sugars.

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:06.040
Those with lower
(but still elevated)

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:08.960
blood sugars,
and those under a BMI of 35

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:10.600
did not have their diabetes risk

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:12.680
significantly dampened
by the drug.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:14.160
One small study even found that,

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:15.880
while metformin alleviated

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:17.760
the insulin resistance
of diabetics

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.120
and those with
a family history of diabetes,

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:23.240
metformin actually worsened
the insulin resistance

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:25.720
(the cause of prediabetes
and type 2 diabetes)

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:28.000
of nondiabetic obese individuals

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:31.280
that didn't have
a genetic predisposition.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:33.720
If you have diabetes,
it can make things better;

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:36.880
if you don't,
it may make things worse.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:38.200
So, healthier individuals

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:40.360
may not reap the benefits
of metformin

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:42.080
that some try to extrapolate

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:45.160
from those
longevity studies on diabetics.

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:46.560
In fact, even those researchers

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:48.880
are explicit that
they don't think metformin

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:50.120
is ready for prime time

00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:51.840
as an anti-aging intervention

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:54.560
by the general population.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:57.840
What's safer
and works even better?

00:16:57.840 --> 00:16:59.440
There was a third arm

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:02.040
of the Diabetes Prevention
Program study.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:03.720
Thousands of prediabetics
were randomized

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:05.240
to metformin or placebo

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:08.040
or lifestyle changes--

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.400
a cholesterol-lowering
low-fat diet and exercise,

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:11.800
at least about 20 minutes a day

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:15.160
moderate intensity exercise,
such as brisk walking.

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:17.960
Compared to the placebo group,
those taking metformin

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:21.080
were 31 percent less likely
to develop diabetes.

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:24.640
The lifestyle group
was 58 percent less likely.

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:26.320
Making simple lifestyle changes

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:28.640
was nearly
twice as effective as metformin

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:30.760
(and with fewer side effects--

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:32.560
for example,
six times fewer cases

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:35.240
of gastrointestinal distress).

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:36.720
Other randomized
controlled trials

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:37.720
have found the same thing--

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:39.920
lifestyle approaches superior

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:42.680
to drug-based approaches
for diabetes prevention.

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:46.120
And, that 60 or so percent
drop in risk was not among those

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:48.480
who actually made
the lifestyle change,

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:51.240
just those instructed to do so.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:53.040
Another diabetes
prevention trial

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:54.720
published in the New England
Journal of Medicine

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:56.720
that found the same
58 percent reduction

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:58.580
in diabetes risk
among those asked

00:17:58.580 --> 00:17:59.840
to make lifestyle changes

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.720
drilled down to see
what the benefit was

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:03.960
for those who fully complied.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:05.280
Only a fraction

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:06.920
of the lifestyle
intervention group

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:08.760
met the modest goals.

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:09.800
Only about a third

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:11.800
increased their exercise,
for example.

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:12.960
Only about a quarter

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.720
met the minimum
recommended intake of fiber

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:18.120
(found concentrated
in whole plant foods)

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:20.520
or cut down
on enough saturated fat

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:23.440
(mostly dairy, dessert,
chicken, and pork).

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:26.240
But, they did better
than the control group,

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:28.760
and fewer of them developed
diabetes because of it.

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:31.240
But, what if you look
just at the folks

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:32.600
who actually made all

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:34.080
(or even just 4 out of 5)

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:36.080
of the recommended
lifestyle changes?

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:38.760
They had zero diabetes--

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:41.600
none of them got diabetes
over the course of the study,

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:46.120
meaning effectively
a 100 percent drop in risk.

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:48.480
